     1,
     "Hi - I have tracks of elephant seals out in the North pacific that I&#39;m\r<br>trying to plot base maps for. I&#39;ve tried using borders() to provide\r<br>some spatial reference, but I&#39;m having two problems. If I just use\r<br>coord_cartesian() one of the tracks ranges over 180 longitude, so the\r<br>lines jump and stretch all the way across the map. I also tried using\r<br>coord_map() with a mercator, so that I could set the limits (Hadley&#39;s\r<br>suggestion in an archived post), and while this projects the data, I&#39;m\r<br>getting lots of horizontal lines across the image. lastly, if I try a\r<br>different projection, e.g. albers, or lambert, I get errors within\r<br>mapproj.\r<br>\r<br>What I would like is a projected map with all the data - including the\r<br>one track that spans 180.\r<br>\r<br>Following are the script with examples of each scenario, and my\r<br>session info.\r<br>\r<br>Thanks,\r<br>Rob\r<br>\r<br>\r<br>library(ggplot2)\r<br>library(mapproj)\r<br>\r<br>xrng&lt;- c(-180,-110)\r<br>yrng&lt;- c(26,58)\r<br>test &lt;- structure(list(ssm_lon = c(-122.5243555, -124.9194642,\r<br>-126.3239824,\r<br>-127.40948075, -128.5212725, -129.7852279, -130.711364,\r<br>-131.95837465,\r<br>-133.1749459, -134.5870609, -135.79971705, -137.921153,\r<br>-139.40240025,\r<br>-140.1209508, -142.0049869, -142.7794206, -142.8833781, -144.4045273,\r<br>-146.0335032, -147.2940082, -147.0862642, -146.2247927,\r<br>-145.00113375,\r<br>-143.67353945, -142.32815315, -142.1534978, -140.5330504,\r<br>-139.5909128,\r<br>-139.2389819, -139.04089195, -139.5415736, -139.5179058,\r<br>-139.6090339,\r<br>-139.8720339, -140.1074476, -139.8576515, -139.93522385,\r<br>-139.9902051,\r<br>-139.7008326, -139.7594677, -139.6228665, -139.81883185,\r<br>-139.9214362,\r<br>-139.7920991, -139.130565, -138.87754045, -138.3303843, -138.2033562,\r<br>-136.8210148, -135.5121461, -133.74318665, -133.0009083,\r<br>-133.0266113,\r<br>-132.6003989, -131.72282715, -129.8077801, -128.01873